## 3D models, checks and renders
On first use save the block below as `bs3d.py` (stdlib only). It needs the LDraw parts library: it looks in `LDRAW_DIR`, `/workspace/brick-scout/ldraw`, `/usr/share/ldraw`, `~/ldraw`; if none, `python3 bs3d.py getlib` downloads the official library (146 MB download, about 620 MB unpacked; ask before using that much disk). Custom .dat files next to the model, or in folders listed in `LDRAW_LOCAL` (colon separated), are found too; MPD submodels are expanded.
- `check model.ldr`: stud connections (any direction, so SNOT counts), collisions, unconnected parts, missing part files; exit code 1 on any problem. It checks studs only and prints `NOT CHECKED` for hinges, clips, bars, pins, axles, wheels, minifig parts and any part at a non-90 degree angle: report those as not checked, never as connected. Overlaps between two minifig parts are ignored, not called collisions.
- `stability model.ldr`: the check plus single-stud joints, load-bearing single-stud joints and a centre of mass over footprint test. Call it approximate, not a physics or Studio check.
- Seated minifigs: no seat joint has been verified, so every seated figure is not checked. Snap data for hinges and clips (LDCad shadow library `SNAP_*` metadata) is not used.
- `render model.ldr out.png [lat lon | front|back|left|right|top|bottom|home] [WxH]`: LeoCAD (`--camera-angles` or `--viewpoint`; LeoCAD's `-c` picks a named camera inside the model, not an angle). Uses xvfb-run when there is no display.
- `spin model.ldr out.mp4 [frames 60] [fps 20]`: LeoCAD frames around the model plus ffmpeg (libx264, yuv420p, crf 18), about 1 s per frame.
- Without LeoCAD or ffmpeg these print what is missing and exit 2: say so, and offer the .ldr for Studio or LeoCAD on the user's computer.
- Run `bs.py ldrcount model.ldr parts.csv` before stating a piece count. A Rebrickable minifig torso assembly (973c..) is 5 LDraw parts and legs (970c..) are 3.
- `check` is for positioned models; the flat grid `.ldr` from `export` will show every part unconnected.
- Reusable code lives in this skill's code blocks so it travels with the skill; put a new script or fix in `PROPOSED.md` next to the skill for review.
- If saving a skill change fails, write it to `PROPOSED.md` next to the skill and tell the user the path; do not retry in a loop.

